  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 100 PM CDT TUE OCT 13 2026\n\nMid to upper level ridging will remain centered to our southwest \ntoday and Wednesday, then will move northeast over the area on \nThursday ahead of a longwave trough that will dive southeast over \nthe Northern Plains and will swing east over the Great Lakes and \nOhio River Valley Region by Friday. Elongated surface high \npressure currently extends from across the Northern Gulf Coast \nnortheast into the Southern Appalachians and will migrate \nsoutheast through late week, expending from across Southern \nAlabama eastward to offshore of the Southeast Atlantic Coast as \nstrong high pressure builds south into the Northern Plains and a \ncold front advances southeast across the Midwest and Ohio River \nValley Region.\n\nHigh temperatures through late week will near the established \nrecords for this time of year as listed in the climate section of \nthis discussion. The temperature difference will be great between \nthe cool overnights and the very warm afternoons thanks to lower \nhumidity and mostly sunny skies through late week. The cold front \nwill move into the area Friday afternoon and evening in mostly dry\nfashion due to the lack of moisture return ahead of this system \nalong with much of the support aloft being located well to the \nnortheast of the area. Behind the front, strong and expansive \nsurface high pressure will build into much of the Plains, Deep \nSouth, Midwest and Ohio River Valley Regions over the weekend with\na strong cool air advection regime.\n\n05",
